---
import { u } from '../lib/url';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import PalmLeaf from '../components/PalmLeaf.astro';
---
<Base
  ogImage="/og/about.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="A verse of the Shiva Swarodaya on a palm leaf"
  title="About the Shiva Swarodaya"
  description="What Swara Yoga is, what the Shiva Swarodaya teaches, what modern research does and does not show, and why this site exists. Taught by Ma Shakti Devpriya."
>
  <PageIntro
    sanskrit="शिवस्वरोदय"
    eyebrow="About"
    title="Shiva Swarodaya"
    lede="The tantric scripture in which Shiva reveals to Parvati the knowledge of Swara, the flow of breath through which Prana moves. It is the foundation of Swara Yoga."
  />

  <section class="section leaf-section" style="padding-top: 8px">
    <div class="container">
      <PalmLeaf
        lines={['स्वरज्ञानात्परं गुह्यं स्वरज्ञानात्परं धनम् ।', 'स्वरज्ञानात्परं ज्ञानं न वा दृष्टं न वा श्रुतम् ॥']}
        number="॥ २१ ॥"
      >
        <p class="translit"><i>svarajñānāt paraṁ guhyaṁ svarajñānāt paraṁ dhanam |<br />svarajñānāt paraṁ jñānaṁ na vā dṛṣṭaṁ na vā śrutam ||</i></p>
        <p class="meaning">“There is no secret higher than the knowledge of Swara, and no wealth higher than the knowledge of Swara. A knowledge higher than the knowledge of Swara has never been seen or heard.”</p>
        <p class="cite">Shiva Swarodaya, verse 21 in the edition with the commentary of Pt. Mihir Chandra (Mumbai: Khemraj Shrikrishnadas, Shri Venkateshwar Press). Verse numbers differ between editions. English rendering ours.</p>
      </PalmLeaf>
    </div>
  </section>

  <nav class="container" aria-label="On this page" style="margin-bottom: 8px">
    <ul class="toc">
      <li><a href="#text">The scripture</a></li>
      <li><a href="#guru">Received from the Guru</a></li>
      <li><a href="#swara">What is Swara?</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ideas">Main ideas</a></li>
      <li><a href="#science">What science shows</a></li>
      <li><a href="#purpose">Why this site</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <article class="article">
    <section class="section" id="text">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>The scripture</h2>
        <p>The <strong>Shiva Swarodaya</strong> (Śiva Svarodaya) is a Sanskrit scripture of the tantric tradition. It opens as a dialogue: Parvati asks Shiva for the knowledge that brings every accomplishment, and Shiva, the first teacher of Yoga, answers by revealing the science of Swara. Its author and date are not known. It has been preserved and taught within the tradition for generations.</p>
        <p>Few texts speak of their own subject so highly. The Shiva Swarodaya calls the knowledge of Swara the highest secret, the highest wealth and the highest knowledge, and places all learning within it. In Ram Kumar Rai's translation:</p>
        <blockquote class="verse">
          <p>“All the Vedas, Sastras, Music, and all the three worlds are inherent in the Svaras and the Svaras are identical with Atman.”</p>
          <cite>Shiva Swarodaya, tr. Ram Kumar Rai, <i>Śivasvarodaya: Text with English Translation</i> (Varanasi: Prachya Prakashan). Verse number to be confirmed against the printed edition.</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>The scripture describes the nadis, the three flows of breath, the five tattvas that arise within each flow, and how every kind of action is best undertaken in harmony with them. It also covers health, timing and the signs of the body, including signs traditionally linked to the approach of death.</p>
        <p>Editions differ. The Bihar School of Yoga edition by Swami Muktibodhananda has 395 verses, and verse numbers vary between editions. Where we quote the text on this site, we name the edition we used.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section alt" id="guru">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>A teaching received from the Guru</h2>
        <p>Swara Yoga is a tantric discipline. Its practices are not meant for self-study from books or videos. They are received from a qualified teacher, who gives them in the right order, at the right time, and watches over the student's practice.</p>
        <p>The Shiva Swarodaya itself says to whom this knowledge should be given:</p>
        <PalmLeaf
          lines={['शान्ते शुद्धे सदाचारे गुरुभक्त्येकमानसे ।', 'दृढचित्ते कृतज्ञे च देयं चैव स्वरोदयम् ॥']}
          number="॥ १३ ॥"
        >
          <p class="leaf-meaning">“Swarodaya should be given to one who is peaceful, pure, of good conduct, whose mind is wholly devoted to the Guru, who is steady of mind and grateful.”</p>
          <p class="leaf-cite">Shiva Swarodaya, verse 13 (Khemraj Shrikrishnadas edition, with the commentary of Pt. Mihir Chandra). English rendering ours.</p>
        </PalmLeaf>
        <p>For this reason, this site explains the teaching and offers only simple, safe observation of the natural breath. The practices themselves are taught by Ma Shakti Devpriya in her courses, in stages, where each step can be guided and every question answered.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="swara">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>What is Swara?</h2>
        <p>In Sanskrit, <em>swara</em> means sound or tone. In Swara Yoga it has a particular meaning: the flow of breath through the nostrils, and especially which nostril is flowing more freely at a given moment.</p>
        <p>Most of the time, one nostril is more open than the other. After a while the flow shifts to the other side. Now and then both flow evenly. The tradition treats these changes as the visible sign of something subtler: the movement of Prana, the life force, through the channels of the body.</p>
        <p><em>Swarodaya</em> means “the rising of the Swara”. Swara Yoga is the discipline of observing this rising, understanding it, and in time, under guidance, learning to work with it.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Swara Yoga is not a breathing exercise</h3>
          <p>Pranayama regulates the breath through set techniques. Modern breathwork uses the breath to produce a particular state. Swara Yoga begins elsewhere. It first asks you to notice the breath as it already is, and to read what that flow indicates. Technique comes later, from the teacher, and within a traditional framework.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section alt" id="ideas">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>Main ideas</h2>
        <span class="kind">From the text</span>
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ida, Pingala and Sushumna</h3>
        <p>The text names three principal nadis. Ida is connected with the left nostril and has a lunar, cooling quality. Pingala is connected with the right nostril and has a solar, heating quality. Sushumna is the central channel, active when both nostrils flow together.</p>
        <blockquote class="verse">
          <p>“Ida is situated on the left (side of the spinal cord), Pingala on the right and Susumna in the middle (of the Spinal cord).”</p>
          <cite>Shiva Swarodaya, tr. Ram Kumar Rai. Verse number to be confirmed.</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>The rhythm of the day</h3>
        <p>According to the text, the flow of breath alternates between the two sides through the day and night, changing roughly every two and a half <em>ghatis</em>. A ghati is 24 minutes, so this is about an hour. In practice the rhythm varies from person to person and day to day, which is part of what a student learns to observe.</p>

        <h3>The five tattvas</h3>
        <p>Within each flow, the text describes the five elements (earth, water, fire, air and space) arising in turn. Each is said to have its own character, and the text links them to different kinds of activity. This is one of the subtler parts of the teaching, learned under the guidance of a teacher.</p>

        <h3>Action in harmony with the flow</h3>
        <p>A large part of the text matches activities to the flow of breath. Broadly, the lunar flow is associated with calm, steady and receptive activity, the solar flow with active and demanding work, and Sushumna with meditation and inner practice. The purpose is to act in harmony with one's own rhythm, not to follow rules mechanically.</p>

        <p class="kind" style="margin-top: 20px">Interpretation</p>
        <p>Read as a whole, the Shiva Swarodaya is less about nostrils than about awareness. The breath is visible and Prana is subtle. Swara is the doorway between the two: by watching the breath closely, a practitioner gradually becomes sensitive to the movement of Prana and to the tattvas, and through them to their own mind.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="science">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>What modern research shows</h2>
        <span class="kind science">Modern science</span>
        <p>The alternation of airflow between the nostrils is well documented. Physicians call it the <strong>nasal cycle</strong>. It was first described in medical literature by the German physician Richard Kayser in 1895. It is controlled by the autonomic nervous system, which alternately swells and shrinks the tissue inside each side of the nose.</p>
        <p>A 2018 review of the research reports that about 70 to 80 percent of healthy adults show a nasal cycle, and that one phase can last anywhere from 30 minutes to 6 hours. A strictly regular, alternating pattern is seen in a smaller share of people.</p>
        <p>Some researchers have linked the nasal cycle to alternating activity in the two hemispheres of the brain, notably Werntz and colleagues in 1983. Later reviews treat this as an interesting hypothesis that still needs more research, not as settled fact.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <h3>What this does and does not mean</h3>
          <p>Research confirms that the breath does alternate between the nostrils, as the tradition describes. It does not test the traditional teachings about Prana, the tattvas, or the right timing of actions, and it should not be read as proving them. Swara Yoga is not a medical treatment. If you have a health condition, please consult a doctor.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section alt" id="purpose">
      <div class="container prose">
        <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
        <p>Swara Yoga is often presented today as a set of quick tricks, or mixed with modern breathwork until the original teaching is hard to find. The purpose of this site is to honour the Shiva Swarodaya and to share the authentic teaching of Swara Yoga, faithfully and in plain language, as it lives in the tradition of Ma Shakti Devpriya.</p>
        <p>We work to a few simple principles:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>What the text says is kept separate from later interpretation and from modern science.</li>
          <li>Every important claim has a source.</li>
          <li>Understanding comes before technique, and practice before promises.</li>
          <li>No medical claims, and no shortcuts.</li>
          <li>Practices are learned from the teacher, not from the page.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>You can begin with the free seven-day course, <em>Meet your Swara</em>, and continue with the foundation course when you are ready.</p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={u('/courses/meet-your-swara')}>Begin “Meet your Swara”</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={u('/lineage')}>The lineage</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="ma">
      <div class="container ma">
        <img src={u('/images/ma-portrait.webp')} srcset={`${u('/images/ma-portrait-300.webp')} 300w, ${u('/images/ma-portrait.webp')} 520w`} sizes="(max-width: 640px) 220px, 260px" alt="Ma Shakti Devpriya" width="520" height="605" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <div class="prose">
          <p class="eyebrow">The teacher</p>
          <h2>Ma Shakti Devpriya</h2>
          <p>Ma Shakti Devpriya, known to her students as Ma Shakti or Guruma, is a co-founder of The Shakti Multiversity with her consort Acharya Agyaatadarshan Anand Nath. She is <em>poornabhishiktaa</em> in the Sri Vidya Kaula Krama tradition and has been immersed in Tantra since 2006. She began teaching publicly in late 2012, with the permission of her Gurudev, Shri Shivanand Nath ji.</p>
          <p>Swara Yoga is central to her teaching, together with the Panchamahabhutas, traditional symbolism (Prachya Prateek Vigyaan) and mandalas. She is the author of <em>Tattva Shakti Vigyaan: Tantra for Modern Man</em>. Her students include yoga teachers, doctors, business leaders and homemakers.</p>
          <p><a href={u('/lineage')}>Read about the lineage →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .leaf-meaning { font: 400 clamp(17px, 1.7vw, 19px)/1.6 var(--font-display); color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .leaf-cite { font: 400 13px/1.5 var(--font-ui); color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  .leaf-section .translit { font: 400 16px/1.7 var(--font-body); color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .leaf-section .meaning { font: 400 clamp(18px, 1.8vw, 20px)/1.6 var(--font-display); color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 10px; }
  .leaf-section .cite { font: 400 13px/1.5 var(--font-ui); color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }
  .verse .dev { font: 400 clamp(19px, 2vw, 22px)/1.7 var(--font-sanskrit); font-style: normal; color: var(--kumkum); margin-bottom: 12px; }
  .verse .en { font-style: italic; }
  .ma { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 260px 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 5vw, 56px); align-items: center; }
  .ma img { width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 4 / 5; object-fit: cover; object-position: 50% 20%; border-radius: 999px 999px 14px 14px; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .ma { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .ma img { width: min(220px, 64%); } }
</style>
